    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm having trouble visualizing the actual movement of charge. Like, in the AC version, since the current is constantly switching directions, what is actually happening inside the dielectric? Is the charge just sloshing back and forth or is it doing something else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine the capacitor as two plates separated by a dielectric, and the charge is trying to build up on those plates. In an AC circuit, the voltage source is constantly flipping its polarity, which means the direction of the electric field across the dielectric is also constantly reversing. This reversal forces the charge on the plates to constantly switch its direction of flow—it's not just sloshing; it's being continuously pushed and pulled by the changing external voltage. This constant charging and discharging cycle is what allows the capacitor to pass AC current.",
      "source": "target",
