    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I get the physical movement part. But I was looking at some notes about how the dielectric material reacts to that field. Does the dielectric itself participate in the current flow at all, or is it just sitting there? I'm also a bit confused about why the reactance decreases as frequency goes up—can you walk me through that relationship a bit more?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The dielectric material itself is usually an insulator, meaning it doesn't conduct current in the traditional sense, but it *does* participate by becoming polarized. When the electric field changes, the molecules within the dielectric align themselves, which opposes the change in the field—this is the polarization effect. The reason reactance decreases as frequency increases is because the capacitor has less time to fully charge and discharge between the rapid swings of the AC voltage. If the frequency is very high, the voltage flips before the charges on the plates have time to build up a significant opposing charge, so it acts more like a short circuit (low reactance).",
      "source": "target",
